## Interfaces in MeTube
In TypeScript, an interface defines a **contract**—what properties and methods an object must have, without implementing them. Think of it as the rules every class must follow.

---
## Example (MeTube)
We have different types of content: videos and playlists. We want them both to be **displayable**.

```ts
interface Displayable {
	render(): string;
}
class Video implements Displayable {
	title: string;
	constructor(title: string) {
		this.title = title;
	}
	render(): string {
		return `Video: ${this.title}`;
	}
}
class Playlist implements Displayable {
	name: string;
	constructor(name: string) {		this.name = name;
}
    render(): string {		return `Playlist: ${this.name}`;
	}
}
```

- `interface` defines the shape
- `implements` promises the class follows that shape
- Forget a method? TypeScript catches it!

### Multiple interfaces
```ts
interface Likeable {
    like(): void;
}

class Video implements Displayable, Likeable {
    title: string;
    constructor(title: string) { this.title = title; }
    render(): string { return `Video: ${this.title}`; }
    like(): void { console.log(`${this.title} liked!`); }
}
```
- Classes can only `extend` **one class** but `implement` **many interfaces**

---
## Assignment:
At MeTube, create an interface `DescribableContent` with a method `describe(): string`.

Then implement two classes:
1. **Video** – has `title` (string) and `length` (minutes). `describe()` returns `'Video "<title>" is <length> minutes long'`
2. **Playlist** – has `name` (string) and `videoCount` (number). `describe()` returns `'Playlist "<name>" has <videoCount> videos'`

